          "question": "I am 52 and my father had a heart attack at 55.",
          "answer": "**If you have chest pain, pressure, or tightness, shortness of breath, pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back, sweating, or feeling faint, call emergency services now. Do not drive yourself.** Your message did not describe any of these, but if you ever have them, do not wait.\n\nThe guidance I could retrieve addresses your family history only in part. In the lipid screening guidance (*Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation*, major cardiology society consensus summary), a family history of premature cardiovascular disease is listed as a \"risk-enhancing feature.\" It is not a diagnosis. The guidance says these features can shift a decision for people whose estimated 10-year risk falls in the borderline or intermediate range. That estimate uses age, sex, cholesterol, blood pressure, diabetes, and smoking status. The passage I found does not define \"premature\" by an exact age, and it does not say what your own risk is.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not cover what to do next for someone in your situation. A reasonable step is to ask a clinician to estimate your 10-year cardiovascular risk and to discuss whether your father's history changes your screening plan.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
